1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,500
Concrete bunkers.

2
00:00:03,501 --> 00:00:05,800
Huge guns.

3
00:00:05,801 --> 00:00:10,240
A gauntlet of piercing, blocking, and exploding obstacles,

4
00:00:10,241 --> 00:00:13,240
plus thousands of well-armed German soldiers.

5
00:00:13,241 --> 00:00:15,640
The only way to defeat Hitler's Germany

6
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:18,960
was to breach this daunting line of defenses.

7
00:00:18,961 --> 00:00:23,360
Now, the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:42,920
Today, scattered along the coast of Western Europe lie

9
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:45,280
remnants of the vast fortifications erected during

10
00:00:45,380 --> 00:00:48,880
World War II to protect Hitler's Germany against an

11
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,120
Allied invasion.

12
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:54,960
In France, along the Normandy coast,

13
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:58,700
the beaches are quiet now, except for the winds that blow

14
00:00:58,701 --> 00:01:01,040
blow off the English Channel and the waves that brush

15
00:01:01,140 --> 00:01:02,040
against the shore.

16
00:01:04,740 --> 00:01:06,940
Guns and tanks that remain are rusted,

17
00:01:07,040 --> 00:01:09,840
are on display in museums that safeguard the memory of what

18
00:01:09,940 --> 00:01:14,540
happened here on June 6, 1944, D-Day.

19
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:21,860
It's the most important day of the 20th century.

20
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:25,700
And everything before that led up to D-Day.

21
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:28,800
Everything since has been a consequence of what happened

22
00:01:28,900 --> 00:01:30,840
on D-Day.

23
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:33,600
It was the day that the Allied armies of America,

24
00:01:33,700 --> 00:01:36,740
Britain, and Canada breached the barrier that made an entire

25
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:40,740
continent a fortress, Hitler's Atlantic Wall.

26
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:44,160
The Atlantic Wall was one of the great walls of history.

27
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:45,360
There are many great walls.

28
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:49,060
The Great Wall of China, the Roman Wall, which walled off

29
00:01:49,160 --> 00:01:51,400
the barbarians from the Roman Empire,

30
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:55,700
and then the long, long wall that Hitler built from Norway

31
00:01:55,701 --> 00:01:58,740
the entire Atlantic coast to try to block Europe off

32
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:01,940
from the Americans and the British.

33
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:03,840
The Atlantic Wall defenses extended

34
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:06,640
along nearly 3,000 miles of coastline,

35
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:09,040
from the Arctic Circle to the Pyrenees Mountains

36
00:02:09,140 --> 00:02:12,180
along Spain's northern border.

37
00:02:12,280 --> 00:02:14,880
The Atlantic Wall was not a continuous barrier,

38
00:02:14,980 --> 00:02:17,960
but rather a series of fortified locations.

39
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:21,160
Between the man-made defenses, precipitous cliffs

40
00:02:21,260 --> 00:02:23,860
and rocky shorelines provided natural barriers

41
00:02:23,861 --> 00:02:25,460
to keep invaders at bay.

42
00:02:28,160 --> 00:02:31,060
The most imposing features along the Atlantic Wall

43
00:02:31,061 --> 00:02:34,340
were massive concrete bunkers built

44
00:02:34,341 --> 00:02:36,740
to protect the all-important guns installed there

45
00:02:36,741 --> 00:02:38,700
to destroy an approaching invasion fleet.

46
00:02:40,140 --> 00:02:45,200
The heaviest artillery was a 40.6-centimeter gun.

47
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:50,680
There were several of them, seven in Norway and three in France.

48
00:02:50,681 --> 00:02:54,760
There were Navy guns shooting quite precise

49
00:02:54,761 --> 00:02:58,020
over a range of maximum 30, 35 miles.

50
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:03,760
Bunkers were also designed to serve as observation posts,

51
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:07,880
ammunition stockpiles, communication centers,

52
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:11,480
troops' quarters, and to store supplies and food

53
00:03:11,481 --> 00:03:13,400
to sustain the men stationed on the wall.

54
00:03:16,260 --> 00:03:19,200
Here, south of Ostend in Belgium,

55
00:03:19,201 --> 00:03:23,200
lies a typical stretch of Atlantic wall construction.

56
00:03:23,201 --> 00:03:28,200
What you see there is the fire control post of a coastal battery.

57
00:03:28,201 --> 00:03:34,200
What you see here is an emplacement with an anti-aircraft gun.

58
00:03:34,201 --> 00:03:39,200
And very special because it's still there.

59
00:03:39,201 --> 00:03:42,200
A system of covered trenches.

60
00:03:42,201 --> 00:03:46,200
These are normal trenches but covered, protecting soldiers

61
00:03:46,201 --> 00:03:48,900
running forth in these corridors,

62
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:54,440
and also protecting the site from the air.

63
00:03:54,540 --> 00:03:56,280
To help obscure the bunkers and guns

64
00:03:56,380 --> 00:03:58,220
from Allied aerial reconnaissance,

65
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:02,120
the Germans blanketed them with camouflage netting.

66
00:04:02,220 --> 00:04:05,820
From the air, Allied observers could see the array of obstacles

67
00:04:05,920 --> 00:04:09,460
designed to block an invasion on the beaches.

68
00:04:09,560 --> 00:04:11,700
But hidden beneath the water's surface

69
00:04:11,800 --> 00:04:16,200
often lurked a menacing gauntlet of death and destruction.

70
00:04:16,201 --> 00:04:23,200
During the war the challenge for the Germans was to block every possible attempt of invasion by the Allies.

71
00:04:23,201 --> 00:04:28,200
And for that purpose they didn't only build thousands of bunkers,

72
00:04:28,201 --> 00:04:36,200
but they covered literally the beach with hundreds of thousands of beach obstacles.

73
00:04:36,201 --> 00:04:43,200
Not only obstacles, but also thousands of kilometres of boatwire has been used on the Atlantic Wall.

74
00:04:43,201 --> 00:04:45,700
between the bunkers, between all the fields.

75
00:04:45,800 --> 00:04:47,140
And do not forget the mines.

76
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:50,000
About more than 6 million mines have been placed

77
00:04:50,100 --> 00:04:52,140
all over the coast.

78
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,080
The Atlantic Wall was a fortification

79
00:04:54,180 --> 00:04:57,820
of such magnitude that only the most ambitious and audacious

80
00:04:57,920 --> 00:05:01,180
attack in the history of warfare could possibly defeat it.

81
00:05:03,720 --> 00:05:06,460
During the first few years of World War II,

82
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:09,220
German defeat seemed almost impossible.

83
00:05:09,320 --> 00:05:13,200
Adolf Hitler's war machine appeared unstoppable.

84
00:05:13,201 --> 00:05:16,640
In the 1930s, the Fuhrer had seemed an answered prayer

85
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:20,140
to a Germany left battered and bankrupt after World War I.

86
00:05:23,580 --> 00:05:27,820
Hitler promised a way out of despair through conquest.

87
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:33,320
In 1939, he unleashed his army against the nations of Europe.

88
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:36,460
In March, Czechoslovakia fell.

89
00:05:36,560 --> 00:05:40,260
In September, Hitler's forces poured into Poland.

90
00:05:40,261 --> 00:05:43,460
Britain and France at last declared war on Germany,

91
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:45,760
but they could do little to halt the Nazi blitzkrieg.

92
00:05:48,760 --> 00:05:52,900
Hitler's armies marched almost unchecked in the first years,

93
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:56,640
starting with Poland, cleared his eastern frontier,

94
00:05:56,740 --> 00:05:59,920
then Scandinavia, Denmark, and Norway.

95
00:06:00,020 --> 00:06:03,520
From there on into the low countries in France,

96
00:06:03,620 --> 00:06:06,580
most of Western Europe fell under its sway.

97
00:06:06,581 --> 00:06:10,280
If you had penetrated the front, you would roll and roll

98
00:06:10,380 --> 00:06:11,820
and roll and roll.

99
00:06:11,920 --> 00:06:19,220
And it was almost impossible for your opponent to stop you.

100
00:06:19,320 --> 00:06:22,460
After a year of conquests, all that separated Britain

101
00:06:22,560 --> 00:06:25,040
from Nazi domination were the frigid waters

102
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,060
of the English Channel.

103
00:06:27,160 --> 00:06:31,940
But for England, the channel was still in effective moat.

104
00:06:32,040 --> 00:06:36,580
In 1940, to prepare the way for an invasion of Britain,

105
00:06:36,581 --> 00:06:42,080
Hitler's Luftwaffe attempted to bomb England into submission,

106
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,720
raining death from the skies in the Battle of Britain.

107
00:06:49,920 --> 00:06:52,560
Germany's elite air force outnumbered Britain's more

108
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:54,100
than three to one.

109
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:57,300
But 700 British fighter planes fiercely maintained air

110
00:06:57,400 --> 00:06:58,900
supremacy over England.

111
00:06:59,000 --> 00:07:03,540
And after four months, Hitler gave up his plans of invasion.

112
00:07:03,541 --> 00:07:08,220
Then he had the choice, attack the English supply system

113
00:07:08,221 --> 00:07:10,220
with everything that he had, or should he

114
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:13,340
turn upon his other potential enemy, Russia.

115
00:07:13,341 --> 00:07:16,720
He chose Russia.

116
00:07:16,721 --> 00:07:21,020
In June 1941, German forces flooded east toward the Russian

117
00:07:21,021 --> 00:07:23,560
border to begin the assault.

118
00:07:23,561 --> 00:07:26,460
But Hitler now had thousands of miles of Western European

119
00:07:26,461 --> 00:07:29,260
coastline to protect.

120
00:07:29,261 --> 00:07:31,460
How could he defend so much territory,

121
00:07:31,461 --> 00:07:33,800
with most of his troops fighting on the Eastern Front.

122
00:07:37,200 --> 00:07:39,360
Expecting to defeat Russia quickly,

123
00:07:39,361 --> 00:07:43,400
Hitler planned to defend Europe with massive artillery.

124
00:07:43,401 --> 00:07:46,400
The Germans positioned huge guns around the major ports

125
00:07:46,401 --> 00:07:49,820
of Belgium, Holland, and France.

126
00:07:49,821 --> 00:07:53,340
But in the East, the Russians fought valiantly.

127
00:07:53,341 --> 00:07:55,160
The Germans were stuck in Russia,

128
00:07:55,161 --> 00:07:58,220
bracing for the approaching Russian winter.

129
00:07:58,221 --> 00:08:01,260
As Hitler ordered more troops to subdue the Soviets,

130
00:08:01,261 --> 00:08:04,500
He needed a new plan to protect the Western Front.

131
00:08:04,501 --> 00:08:07,860
His solution was a wall that would defend every mile of

132
00:08:07,861 --> 00:08:11,800
coastline from Denmark to Spain.

133
00:08:11,801 --> 00:08:14,900
As the war progressed, Hitler's concept of the wall

134
00:08:14,901 --> 00:08:20,280
would grow from a modest defense to an impregnable fortress.

135
00:08:20,281 --> 00:08:23,240
What could Germans do more than defend it

136
00:08:23,241 --> 00:08:27,120
with block houses, batteries, everything?

137
00:08:27,121 --> 00:08:28,860
There were no troops enough.

138
00:08:28,861 --> 00:08:32,040
There were no armored divisions enough.

139
00:08:32,041 --> 00:08:34,340
They could make concrete shelters.

140
00:08:34,341 --> 00:08:35,200
They had labors.

141
00:08:35,201 --> 00:08:38,920
They had materials to construct those things.

142
00:08:38,921 --> 00:08:43,040
The Germans had no other choice than defend Europe

143
00:08:43,041 --> 00:08:45,660
with an Atlantic one.

144
00:08:45,661 --> 00:08:47,460
Hitler gave the order.

145
00:08:47,461 --> 00:08:50,940
The challenge was how to execute it with limited resources

146
00:08:50,941 --> 00:08:54,520
in occupied territory in the middle of a war.

147
00:08:54,521 --> 00:08:57,200
The Germans had been in World War I,

148
00:08:57,201 --> 00:08:59,540
And they knew how to dig ditches.

149
00:08:59,541 --> 00:09:02,700
And they knew how to put trenches in place.

150
00:09:02,701 --> 00:09:06,380
And they knew the kind of fire they needed to hold a position.

151
00:09:06,381 --> 00:09:08,040
And they put all of that to use.

152
00:09:10,480 --> 00:09:13,260
The first goal was to fortify ports

153
00:09:13,261 --> 00:09:17,480
that could be used by the Allies to land troops, divisions,

154
00:09:17,481 --> 00:09:18,380
armies.

155
00:09:18,381 --> 00:09:20,420
So from the northern part of Holland

156
00:09:20,421 --> 00:09:24,060
to the southern part of France, the major ports and U-boat

157
00:09:24,061 --> 00:09:26,600
bases were fortified.

158
00:09:26,601 --> 00:09:29,240
The Germans concentrated their early construction efforts

159
00:09:29,241 --> 00:09:33,600
on the French coast, off a strait called the Pas de Calais,

160
00:09:33,601 --> 00:09:36,780
where the English Channel was narrowest.

161
00:09:36,781 --> 00:09:38,220
From a practical standpoint, this

162
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:42,080
was the most likely route for the Allied invasion force.

163
00:09:42,081 --> 00:09:45,180
From Calais, the Allies could march straight to Germany,

164
00:09:45,181 --> 00:09:49,320
only a few hundred miles away.

165
00:09:49,321 --> 00:09:52,900
On December 7, 1941, as the Japanese

166
00:09:52,901 --> 00:09:55,800
launched its devastating attack on Pearl Harbor,

167
00:09:55,801 --> 00:10:01,040
changed abruptly, and enraged America declared war on Japan.

168
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:04,040
Three days later, Hitler declared war on the United

169
00:10:04,140 --> 00:10:05,940
States, and we don't know why.

170
00:10:06,040 --> 00:10:08,440
Nothing made him to it, but he did it.

171
00:10:08,540 --> 00:10:11,880
And when he did it, he was making a bet that the Hitler

172
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:16,960
youth are going to always out-fight those soft,

173
00:10:17,060 --> 00:10:19,820
effeminate Americans who have been brought up in the Boy

174
00:10:19,920 --> 00:10:20,960
Scouts.

175
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:23,560
Although many Americans were anxious to strike back

176
00:10:23,561 --> 00:10:27,060
back at Japan, the United States and its new allied partners

177
00:10:27,160 --> 00:10:31,200
agreed to make Germany's defeat the first priority.

178
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:34,100
The basic strategy was laid out within a month or so

179
00:10:34,200 --> 00:10:36,000
after Pearl Harbor.

180
00:10:36,100 --> 00:10:38,900
And that was Europe first, cross the Channel,

181
00:10:39,000 --> 00:10:40,140
go to the heart of Germany.

182
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:43,420
That was our basic strategy from the very beginning.

183
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:49,920
In August 1942, the Allies launched a raid on the French

184
00:10:50,020 --> 00:10:52,660
coast at the port of Dieppe, in part

185
00:10:52,661 --> 00:10:58,720
in part to appease the Russians, who were insisting that their Western allies waste no time opening a second European front.

186
00:10:59,200 --> 00:11:04,760
The Dieppe Raid, executed by 6,100 Canadian, British, and American troops,

187
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:09,100
was designed as a one-day attack to inflict enough damage on the coastal defenses

188
00:11:09,101 --> 00:11:12,420
to draw at least some Germans away from the Eastern Front.

189
00:11:13,300 --> 00:11:18,060
It turned into an object lesson, revealing their unreadiness to launch a major invasion.

190
00:11:19,620 --> 00:11:22,640
The Dieppe Raid was a trial for the Allied forces.

191
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:25,300
and something of a surprise for the Germans.

192
00:11:25,301 --> 00:11:31,200
They expected a raid, a landing, but not of that size

193
00:11:31,201 --> 00:11:34,760
and not one that was ill-prepared like that.

194
00:11:37,120 --> 00:11:39,780
The attack on the heavily fortified port city

195
00:11:39,781 --> 00:11:42,460
resulted in more than 4,000 Allied casualties,

196
00:11:43,420 --> 00:11:46,240
and almost 1,500 Canadians were taken prisoner.

197
00:11:48,300 --> 00:11:49,960
Tanks had bogged down on the shore,

198
00:11:50,520 --> 00:11:52,640
unable to gain traction on a beach not of heart.

199
00:11:52,660 --> 00:11:57,360
packed sand, but loose stones.

200
00:11:57,460 --> 00:12:00,200
The Allies realized that launching a full-scale invasion

201
00:12:00,300 --> 00:12:04,600
against a heavily fortified port could spell disaster.

202
00:12:04,700 --> 00:12:08,480
The only alternative was to design and build new tools that

203
00:12:08,580 --> 00:12:10,640
would enable them to successfully storm Europe's

204
00:12:10,740 --> 00:12:13,580
open beaches.

205
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:17,620
The Germans could be sure of two things in late 1942.

206
00:12:17,720 --> 00:12:20,880
First, the Allies would launch their invasion from England.

207
00:12:20,881 --> 00:12:23,720
And second, it would take months or even years

208
00:12:23,820 --> 00:12:26,120
for them to make the preparations necessary

209
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:28,620
to avoid another Dieppe.

210
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:30,920
This would provide Hitler ample time

211
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:33,320
to build his unreachable Atlantic Wall.

212
00:12:37,420 --> 00:12:39,640
To fortify the Atlantic Wall, the Germans

213
00:12:39,740 --> 00:12:42,640
used more than a million metric tons of steel,

214
00:12:42,740 --> 00:12:46,480
enough to construct 60 skyscrapers, each the size

215
00:12:46,580 --> 00:12:47,940
of the Chrysler Building.

216
00:12:47,941 --> 00:12:50,700
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

217
00:12:55,880 --> 00:12:59,080
We now return to the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

218
00:13:02,880 --> 00:13:07,200
In 1943, the Atlantic Wall became an important first line

219
00:13:07,201 --> 00:13:09,500
of warning and defense for the Germans

220
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:12,300
against the ever-increasing Allied bombing raids flying

221
00:13:12,301 --> 00:13:14,800
closer and closer to Berlin.

222
00:13:14,801 --> 00:13:17,560
While the big guns, aimed at the sea,

223
00:13:17,660 --> 00:13:20,000
waited silently for a naval invasion,

224
00:13:20,100 --> 00:13:23,140
the anti-aircraft guns under the flight paths of the bombers

225
00:13:23,240 --> 00:13:25,540
roared during repeated action.

226
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:29,340
In 1943, when the Allies bombed Germany

227
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:34,760
on a very large scale, a huge amount of Allied bombers

228
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:37,260
came over every evening.

229
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,260
And when they came back from Germany,

230
00:13:40,360 --> 00:13:44,560
they were not allowed to land in England with bombs on board.

231
00:13:44,561 --> 00:13:48,500
so they dropped their bombs, sometimes in sea,

232
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:53,300
and sometimes on the battery itself.

233
00:13:53,400 --> 00:13:55,700
The resulting damage to the exposed artillery

234
00:13:55,800 --> 00:13:58,400
compelled the Germans to launch a new phase of construction

235
00:13:58,500 --> 00:13:59,200
on the wall.

236
00:14:01,740 --> 00:14:04,140
Workers built huge concrete bunkers

237
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:08,320
with walls 3 and 1 1 meters thick, so thick they could

238
00:14:08,420 --> 00:14:12,880
absorb direct bomb hits with virtually no damage.

239
00:14:12,881 --> 00:14:15,680
The Atlantic Wall became the largest construction project

240
00:14:15,780 --> 00:14:17,520
of the entire war.

241
00:14:17,620 --> 00:14:19,020
An awful lot of the Atlantic Wall,

242
00:14:19,120 --> 00:14:22,080
incidentally, was made by Frenchmen who were,

243
00:14:22,180 --> 00:14:25,420
under the point of a gun, working for the Germans,

244
00:14:25,520 --> 00:14:27,720
helping to build this wall, to pour the cement,

245
00:14:27,820 --> 00:14:30,440
to put in the steel reinforcing rods.

246
00:14:30,540 --> 00:14:33,800
A private German firm, Organization TOT,

247
00:14:33,900 --> 00:14:37,040
supervised much of the wall's construction.

248
00:14:37,140 --> 00:14:41,040
Yeah, the TOT organization was a semi-governmental organization,

249
00:14:41,041 --> 00:14:45,440
a contractor, started with the autobahner project,

250
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:49,440
then was called in for the West Wall in Germany,

251
00:14:49,540 --> 00:14:50,980
and after that, the Atlantic Wall.

252
00:14:53,980 --> 00:14:56,720
By mid-1942, construction crews

253
00:14:56,820 --> 00:15:00,020
were pouring more than 200,000 cubic meters of concrete

254
00:15:00,120 --> 00:15:00,820
every month.

255
00:15:04,200 --> 00:15:07,200
The most heavily fortified portion of the entire Atlantic

256
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:10,160
Wall was a pet project of Hitler's.

257
00:15:10,161 --> 00:15:13,660
Ironically, it had little strategic significance.

258
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:43,760
It could be a base for the Allies to jump onto France.

259
00:15:43,761 --> 00:15:48,260
And secondly, it was propaganda because it was the only part

260
00:15:48,261 --> 00:15:49,800
of Great Britain that they occupied.

261
00:15:52,800 --> 00:15:55,240
Once organization TOT finished its construction

262
00:15:55,241 --> 00:15:57,920
on the islands, it was able to give the mainland coastal

263
00:15:57,921 --> 00:15:59,980
defenses its full attention.

264
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:02,880
Its overall output was enormous.

265
00:16:02,881 --> 00:16:05,520
Germans are very good in statistics.

266
00:16:05,521 --> 00:16:10,060
In the total of the Atlantic Wall, some 11,000, 12,000.

267
00:16:10,061 --> 00:16:14,600
were made, heavy bunkers, with a consumption of 10, 11,

268
00:16:14,700 --> 00:16:17,400
12 million cubic meters of concrete.

269
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:22,800
But for Adolf Hitler, the massive fortifications

270
00:16:22,900 --> 00:16:25,100
still weren't enough.

271
00:16:25,200 --> 00:16:26,640
He decided the Atlantic Wall should

272
00:16:26,740 --> 00:16:30,680
consist of 15,000 defensive posts along the coast,

273
00:16:30,780 --> 00:16:34,180
manned by over 300,000 soldiers and backed

274
00:16:34,280 --> 00:16:38,280
by over 150,000 reserves.

275
00:16:38,281 --> 00:16:41,480
But organization TOT anticipated that it would be able to complete

276
00:16:41,580 --> 00:16:47,020
only 6,000 defensive posts by June 1943.

277
00:16:47,120 --> 00:16:49,320
Hitler's goals for troop deployments on the Wall

278
00:16:49,420 --> 00:16:52,720
were also falling short, and units already stationed there

279
00:16:52,820 --> 00:16:56,840
varied greatly in strength, training, and experience.

280
00:16:56,940 --> 00:17:00,240
The major forces of the German army, the young guys,

281
00:17:00,340 --> 00:17:03,140
strong guys, were in action in the desert

282
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:05,340
and on the Eastern Front.

283
00:17:05,440 --> 00:17:07,280
Now, to populate the Atlantic Wall,

284
00:17:07,281 --> 00:17:09,580
they used the older guys and very young guys,

285
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:13,920
and also foreign volunteers like Russians, Italians,

286
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:16,920
and all that kind of people.

287
00:17:17,020 --> 00:17:21,120
Germany's best weaponry was reserved for the Eastern Front.

288
00:17:21,220 --> 00:17:24,300
The artillery of the Atlantic Wall was supplemented

289
00:17:24,400 --> 00:17:25,540
with captured guns.

290
00:17:28,340 --> 00:17:34,140
This is a Belgian gun, 12 centimeter gun,

291
00:17:34,141 --> 00:17:39,440
used by the Belgian army in very small quantities.

292
00:17:39,540 --> 00:17:44,520
And it was captured by the Germans in May 1940.

293
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:47,720
And there was just one problem with these guns.

294
00:17:47,820 --> 00:17:50,820
There was a very limited stock of ammunition

295
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:53,800
for the Germans.

296
00:17:53,900 --> 00:17:55,420
For the German soldiers stationed

297
00:17:55,520 --> 00:17:58,040
along the Atlantic wall, military duties

298
00:17:58,140 --> 00:18:01,200
took a back seat to construction projects.

299
00:18:01,300 --> 00:18:04,140
It's very, very remarkable that in a short time,

300
00:18:04,141 --> 00:18:07,640
The Germans built a lot of constructions,

301
00:18:07,740 --> 00:18:10,980
little bunkers, but also very big bunkers.

302
00:18:11,080 --> 00:18:12,640
It's remarkable.

303
00:18:12,740 --> 00:18:14,780
But maintaining momentum became increasingly

304
00:18:14,880 --> 00:18:16,180
difficult.

305
00:18:16,280 --> 00:18:18,980
The Allied air campaign devastating Germany

306
00:18:19,080 --> 00:18:20,900
forced workers along the Atlantic wall back

307
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:24,000
to the fatherland, where they went to work rebuilding

308
00:18:24,100 --> 00:18:27,600
a crumbling infrastructure.

309
00:18:27,700 --> 00:18:29,500
The Germans suffered a further setback

310
00:18:29,600 --> 00:18:32,140
after the British general Bernard Montgomery

311
00:18:32,141 --> 00:18:35,240
scored an important victory over General Erwin Rommel,

312
00:18:35,241 --> 00:18:40,140
the Desert Fox, in North Africa.

313
00:18:40,141 --> 00:18:43,080
Victorious Allied troops there were a new threat,

314
00:18:43,081 --> 00:18:46,680
poised near France's vulnerable Mediterranean coast.

315
00:18:46,681 --> 00:18:49,500
In response, the Germans stretched their resources

316
00:18:49,501 --> 00:18:55,100
thinner, fortifying some 400 miles of Mediterranean shoreline.

317
00:18:55,101 --> 00:18:58,900
In late 1943, with the possibility of invasion growing

318
00:18:58,901 --> 00:19:02,400
growing with each passing day, Hitler asked Field Marshal

319
00:19:02,401 --> 00:19:05,700
Rommel to evaluate the coastal defenses.

320
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:08,800
My father had among the German generals

321
00:19:08,801 --> 00:19:12,240
the greatest experience in fighting with the British

322
00:19:12,241 --> 00:19:17,060
and American troops. And so he received the order

323
00:19:17,061 --> 00:19:21,560
to inspect the defense facilities. Rommel toured the

324
00:19:21,561 --> 00:19:26,800
wall from Norway to the Pyrenees. He examined mile after mile

325
00:19:26,801 --> 00:19:30,600
bunkers, gun emplacements, fortifications, and found them

326
00:19:30,601 --> 00:19:34,600
woefully inadequate.

327
00:19:34,601 --> 00:19:37,640
Something had to be done, and quickly.

328
00:19:37,641 --> 00:19:42,480
There was too much coastline to protect and too little time.

329
00:19:42,481 --> 00:19:46,280
The race was on to bolster the Atlantic Wall before the Allies

330
00:19:46,281 --> 00:19:50,080
appeared on the horizon.

331
00:19:50,081 --> 00:19:54,720
Dutch architect Jaap Penrat smuggled 400 Jewish men to safety

332
00:19:54,721 --> 00:19:57,060
by convincing the Nazis they were slave laborers

333
00:19:57,160 --> 00:19:59,460
for the Atlantic Wall.

334
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:02,660
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

335
00:20:08,160 --> 00:20:11,480
We now return to the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

336
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:16,880
In early 1944, the Germans began

337
00:20:16,980 --> 00:20:19,780
to bolster the porous Atlantic Wall defenses.

338
00:20:19,880 --> 00:20:23,480
German Field Marshal Erwin Rommel's plans went beyond more

339
00:20:23,481 --> 00:20:28,280
bunkers and guns. Miles and miles of barbed wire

340
00:20:28,380 --> 00:20:32,220
soon ensnared the coast, and a grotesque assortment of

341
00:20:32,320 --> 00:20:35,460
obstacles were placed on the beaches and concealed by high

342
00:20:35,560 --> 00:20:40,500
tides, all designed to destroy, or at least slow down, landing

343
00:20:40,600 --> 00:20:43,860
craft so machine guns and artillery had more time to

344
00:20:43,960 --> 00:20:48,100
decimate the invaders. Now, this is a nutcracker

345
00:20:48,101 --> 00:20:55,100
It's a very simple device assembled from steel girders

346
00:20:55,200 --> 00:20:59,040
and with a piece of rail.

347
00:20:59,140 --> 00:21:03,640
These were placed on the beach between the high tide

348
00:21:03,740 --> 00:21:05,560
and the low tide.

349
00:21:05,660 --> 00:21:09,760
When a boat hit the obstacle, this rail

350
00:21:09,860 --> 00:21:16,160
exploded a large shell that was attached to the device.

351
00:21:16,161 --> 00:21:19,360
Simpler obstacles, nicknamed hedgehogs by the Allies,

352
00:21:19,460 --> 00:21:22,100
were three metal girders bolted together.

353
00:21:22,200 --> 00:21:24,640
They were first made by the Czechs to help protect

354
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:26,840
their border from the Germans.

355
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:30,140
During the war, these hedgehogs were brought over

356
00:21:30,240 --> 00:21:32,740
by the Germans in large quantities

357
00:21:32,840 --> 00:21:36,620
and placed on the beaches along the Atlantic wall.

358
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:43,280
Sometimes they were just meant to stop landing boats.

359
00:21:43,281 --> 00:21:47,080
Sometimes they had mines or other devices attached,

360
00:21:47,180 --> 00:21:50,220
just as these tetrahedra here.

361
00:21:50,320 --> 00:21:53,360
You can find them in concrete.

362
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:59,060
Usually, they had the teller mine attached to the top.

363
00:21:59,160 --> 00:22:01,700
Belgian gates were first used in Belgium

364
00:22:01,800 --> 00:22:03,700
as anti-tank barriers.

365
00:22:03,800 --> 00:22:05,440
The Germans saw the value of making them

366
00:22:05,540 --> 00:22:07,340
part of the Atlantic wall.

367
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:09,600
The almost elegant gate-like design

368
00:22:09,700 --> 00:22:13,280
was actually a sturdy steel construction.

369
00:22:13,281 --> 00:22:16,280
Placed individually, they offered little resistance.

370
00:22:16,380 --> 00:22:19,680
But when linked together by the coupling hinges on each side,

371
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:22,320
they created an effective barrier against troops

372
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:25,520
and armored vehicles.

373
00:22:25,620 --> 00:22:28,560
Rollers at the base of the gates allowed the barrier as a whole

374
00:22:28,660 --> 00:22:31,460
to flex instead of break and withstand

375
00:22:31,560 --> 00:22:35,060
a tremendous amount of force.

376
00:22:35,160 --> 00:22:38,940
In their desperate effort to block or blow up any invader,

377
00:22:39,040 --> 00:22:41,640
the Germans added a thicket of the most primitive obstacles

378
00:22:41,641 --> 00:22:42,840
imaginable.

379
00:22:42,940 --> 00:22:47,980
The Germans literally raided the woods in Belgium and France.

380
00:22:48,080 --> 00:22:51,580
And thousands of these wooden posts

381
00:22:51,680 --> 00:22:53,280
were placed on the beaches.

382
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:58,400
On top of it, usually they placed a teller mine or a shell.

383
00:22:58,500 --> 00:23:02,900
And some of these devices could even rip off the hull

384
00:23:03,000 --> 00:23:05,200
of the boat.

385
00:23:05,300 --> 00:23:09,240
At the end of 1943, about the same time Rommel was assigned

386
00:23:09,241 --> 00:23:10,740
to the Atlantic Wall.

387
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:13,540
The Allies were deciding who should lead the unprecedented

388
00:23:13,640 --> 00:23:15,780
effort to breach it.

389
00:23:15,880 --> 00:23:19,020
The logical choice was General George Marshall, head of the

390
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:20,680
Joint Chiefs of Staff.

391
00:23:20,780 --> 00:23:23,920
But the job of Supreme Allied Commander went instead

392
00:23:24,020 --> 00:23:26,060
to General Dwight Eisenhower.

393
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:29,500
He was given a very simple set of marching orders.

394
00:23:29,600 --> 00:23:31,100
You will enter the continent of Europe

395
00:23:31,200 --> 00:23:33,740
in conjunction with other Allied powers,

396
00:23:33,840 --> 00:23:35,800
straight to the heart of Germany.

397
00:23:35,900 --> 00:23:37,640
Plans for the invasion of Europe,

398
00:23:37,641 --> 00:23:41,280
code-named Overlord, were well underway before Eisenhower's

399
00:23:41,380 --> 00:23:43,880
appointment.

400
00:23:43,980 --> 00:23:47,280
A three-division assault had been proposed that called for an

401
00:23:47,380 --> 00:23:50,420
invasion of the beaches between the ports of Le Havre and

402
00:23:50,520 --> 00:23:51,860
Cherbourg.

403
00:23:54,060 --> 00:23:56,720
Eisenhower, consulting with British General Montgomery,

404
00:23:56,820 --> 00:23:59,500
the commander of the British and Canadian ground forces,

405
00:23:59,600 --> 00:24:03,700
quickly concluded that the plan was inadequate.

406
00:24:03,800 --> 00:24:07,640
Eisenhower and Montgomery had developed a pretty

407
00:24:07,641 --> 00:24:09,780
good standing operating procedure,

408
00:24:09,900 --> 00:24:13,540
and one of the principles is swat a fly with a sledgehammer.

409
00:24:14,140 --> 00:24:17,020
So they took a look at this narrow front.

410
00:24:17,220 --> 00:24:19,120
They said it's not enough, both of them,

411
00:24:19,220 --> 00:24:20,680
but they were absolutely unanimous.

412
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:23,660
There had to be five divisions rather than three.

413
00:24:24,720 --> 00:24:27,020
An equally crucial decision for the Allies

414
00:24:27,021 --> 00:24:28,820
was selecting their invasion site.

415
00:24:29,320 --> 00:24:31,940
The Allies started with reconnaissance

416
00:24:31,941 --> 00:24:34,660
of the whole French and Belgian coast,

417
00:24:35,140 --> 00:24:36,140
aircraft reconnaissance,

418
00:24:36,141 --> 00:24:39,640
And they sent also out small submarines with divers.

419
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:43,620
They came ashore to take little pieces of the sand

420
00:24:43,720 --> 00:24:49,020
to investigate in laboratories to see if the sand was strong

421
00:24:49,120 --> 00:24:52,620
enough to keep tanks and other equipment.

422
00:24:52,720 --> 00:24:55,620
And finally, they've chosen for Normandy

423
00:24:55,720 --> 00:24:58,060
because they are, in some part of it,

424
00:24:58,160 --> 00:25:00,800
very large sandy beaches.

425
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:05,400
Normandy, however, had major disadvantages.

426
00:25:05,401 --> 00:25:08,600
The Allied armada would have to sail across 120 miles

427
00:25:08,700 --> 00:25:12,240
of the English Channel, a distance more than five times

428
00:25:12,340 --> 00:25:15,440
farther than a route to Calais.

429
00:25:15,540 --> 00:25:17,320
And landing at Normandy would also

430
00:25:17,420 --> 00:25:22,480
place the Allies farther from their ultimate target, Germany.

431
00:25:22,580 --> 00:25:25,780
Still, Normandy offered one distinct advantage.

432
00:25:25,880 --> 00:25:28,560
The Germans had not fortified it as heavily as they had other

433
00:25:28,660 --> 00:25:30,860
parts of the Wall.

434
00:25:30,960 --> 00:25:34,100
The Germans assumed that the Allies would need a port

435
00:25:34,101 --> 00:25:37,340
quickly after an invasion in order to support an invasion

436
00:25:37,440 --> 00:25:40,940
deep into the empire of Hitler.

437
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:44,740
The Allies realized that Normandy was not a good place

438
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:48,580
for that, as did Hitler, but they had ideas to compensate

439
00:25:48,680 --> 00:25:49,720
for that.

440
00:25:49,820 --> 00:25:53,080
They could bring their ports with them.

441
00:25:53,180 --> 00:25:55,380
All over the British Isles, engineers

442
00:25:55,480 --> 00:25:58,820
were secretly building huge concrete caissons, floating

443
00:25:58,920 --> 00:26:04,100
piers, and sections of pontoon roadway.

444
00:26:04,101 --> 00:26:07,240
These were the building blocks of portable modular harbors,

445
00:26:07,340 --> 00:26:10,740
codenamed mulberries.

446
00:26:10,840 --> 00:26:13,040
When the invasion began, the pieces

447
00:26:13,140 --> 00:26:15,280
would be towed to Normandy and assembled

448
00:26:15,380 --> 00:26:17,940
into two functional harbors.

449
00:26:18,040 --> 00:26:21,060
I think each one of those artificial harbors

450
00:26:21,160 --> 00:26:28,260
could handle up to 10,000 tons a day over the beaches.

451
00:26:28,360 --> 00:26:30,200
Workers in America's war factories

452
00:26:30,300 --> 00:26:31,860
were busy designing and building

453
00:26:31,861 --> 00:26:35,360
a variety of specialized shallow bottom landing craft

454
00:26:35,460 --> 00:26:37,860
to enable the quick unloading of men and equipment

455
00:26:37,960 --> 00:26:40,860
right up on the beaches.

456
00:26:40,960 --> 00:26:46,900
The largest were 300-foot long landing ship tanks, or LSTs.

457
00:26:47,000 --> 00:26:51,680
Landing ship infantry, or LSI, could hold as many as 150

458
00:26:51,780 --> 00:26:53,680
soldiers.

459
00:26:53,780 --> 00:26:56,180
The smallest craft, which would transport the first wave

460
00:26:56,280 --> 00:27:00,360
of troops, were called Higgins boats.

461
00:27:00,361 --> 00:27:04,360
named after their inventor in Louisiana.

462
00:27:04,460 --> 00:27:07,600
They needed a shallow draft vessel

463
00:27:07,700 --> 00:27:10,440
that they could turn around in a hurry.

464
00:27:10,540 --> 00:27:13,100
Every American who went ashore in North Africa,

465
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:15,040
every American who went ashore in Italy,

466
00:27:15,140 --> 00:27:18,420
and every American who went ashore in the Pacific Theater

467
00:27:18,520 --> 00:27:22,140
did so in a boat built in New Orleans by Andrew Higgins

468
00:27:22,240 --> 00:27:24,380
and Higgins' industry.

469
00:27:24,480 --> 00:27:26,160
Higgins faced the daunting challenge

470
00:27:26,260 --> 00:27:28,120
of filling the enormous order of the boats

471
00:27:28,121 --> 00:27:32,060
of the boats the Allies needed for the invasion.

472
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:37,260
He moved from a factory that had 80 people working there

473
00:27:37,360 --> 00:27:43,060
to 30,000 employees, of whom about half were women.

474
00:27:43,160 --> 00:27:47,480
And he worked seven days a week, 24 hours a day,

475
00:27:47,580 --> 00:27:49,340
to turn out those landing craft.

476
00:27:49,440 --> 00:27:51,680
And by God, he did it.

477
00:27:51,780 --> 00:27:54,320
As American factories produced more and more equipment

478
00:27:54,420 --> 00:27:57,480
for the Allied war effort, German factories and rail

479
00:27:57,481 --> 00:28:01,180
continued to crumble beneath the onslaught of Allied bombers,

480
00:28:01,280 --> 00:28:04,760
crippling their war output.

481
00:28:04,860 --> 00:28:08,020
The Allied air campaign had also forced the Luftwaffe back

482
00:28:08,120 --> 00:28:10,760
into Germany to defend the fatherland cities against

483
00:28:10,860 --> 00:28:12,760
attack.

484
00:28:12,860 --> 00:28:15,460
This meant that the Allies would have almost total air

485
00:28:15,560 --> 00:28:16,860
superiority over France.

486
00:28:19,440 --> 00:28:23,400
Rommel now believed that Germany could never win this war, but

487
00:28:23,500 --> 00:28:26,440
he saw a strong Atlantic wall as a way to force a negotiated

488
00:28:26,441 --> 00:28:30,940
negotiated peace with the Allies.

489
00:28:31,040 --> 00:28:34,080
The landmine was Rommel's favorite weapon.

490
00:28:34,180 --> 00:28:36,380
After using them effectively during the North African

491
00:28:36,480 --> 00:28:40,020
campaign, he ordered more than 50 million mines to be installed

492
00:28:40,120 --> 00:28:41,980
along the Atlantic wall.

493
00:28:42,080 --> 00:28:44,900
On shore, there were many kinds of mines,

494
00:28:45,000 --> 00:28:48,960
and some of them were designed with glass cases so that they

495
00:28:49,060 --> 00:28:51,400
were not picked up by metal detectors.

496
00:28:51,500 --> 00:28:55,500
This sort of problem with mines, non-magnetic mines,

497
00:28:55,501 --> 00:28:57,500
carries right on to the curse of mines

498
00:28:57,600 --> 00:28:59,240
throughout the world today.

499
00:28:59,340 --> 00:29:03,280
They had laid mines, and they had put out barbed wire.

500
00:29:03,380 --> 00:29:05,240
And they had their mortars zeroed in.

501
00:29:05,340 --> 00:29:08,420
Now, the Germans who were there were put to work laying out

502
00:29:08,520 --> 00:29:11,620
these obstacles into the water between the low and the high

503
00:29:11,720 --> 00:29:14,180
tide and preparing for the invasion.

504
00:29:14,280 --> 00:29:18,620
And they were all praying to God that it doesn't come here.

505
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,500
Under Rommel's supervision, much of the wall

506
00:29:21,600 --> 00:29:23,500
was significantly strengthened.

507
00:29:23,501 --> 00:29:27,500
In six months, over 5 million mines were installed,

508
00:29:27,600 --> 00:29:29,940
far short of his target, but triple the number

509
00:29:30,040 --> 00:29:32,740
planted during the years prior to his arrival.

510
00:29:32,840 --> 00:29:36,640
At the end of 1943, Rommel had considered the Atlantic Wall

511
00:29:36,740 --> 00:29:38,940
porous and vulnerable.

512
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:42,660
By the spring of 1944, he was more confident his heavily

513
00:29:42,760 --> 00:29:44,960
refortified barrier would hold.

514
00:29:45,060 --> 00:29:47,960
But its weak spots still troubled him.

515
00:29:48,060 --> 00:29:51,300
In April 1944, Allied planes began destroying

516
00:29:51,301 --> 00:29:55,000
communication and transportation lines near the coast.

517
00:29:55,100 --> 00:29:58,440
A sure sign, an invasion was imminent.

518
00:29:58,540 --> 00:30:00,940
The Germans were not surprised that bombs were falling

519
00:30:01,040 --> 00:30:05,040
around Calais, as they still expected an invasion there.

520
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:08,960
But ominously, the Allies were also dropping bombs on the coast

521
00:30:09,060 --> 00:30:10,060
of Normandy.

522
00:30:12,860 --> 00:30:15,700
The New Orleans D-Day Museum is just blocks away from the

523
00:30:15,800 --> 00:30:17,860
earliest Higgins Boat Factory.

524
00:30:17,861 --> 00:30:20,100
Founded by historian Stephen Ambrose,

525
00:30:20,200 --> 00:30:23,600
the museum houses the world's largest D-Day oral history

526
00:30:23,700 --> 00:30:25,100
collection.

527
00:30:25,200 --> 00:30:28,100
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

528
00:30:34,620 --> 00:30:37,820
We now return to the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

529
00:30:37,821 --> 00:30:39,320
marvels.

530
00:30:47,560 --> 00:30:53,260
In the spring of 1944, Allied bombs battered Western France.

531
00:30:53,360 --> 00:30:56,300
The devastating attacks deeply troubled German Field Marshal

532
00:30:56,400 --> 00:30:58,980
Erwin Rommel, who was spearheading the construction

533
00:30:59,080 --> 00:31:01,780
and defense of the Atlantic Wall.

534
00:31:01,880 --> 00:31:07,420
My father believed that German strategy should change

535
00:31:07,421 --> 00:31:14,960
fundamentally, because he experienced the efficiency

536
00:31:14,961 --> 00:31:18,300
of the Allied Air Force in Africa.

537
00:31:18,301 --> 00:31:20,840
Rommel's deep concerns about Allied air power

538
00:31:20,841 --> 00:31:23,180
put him in direct conflict with German General

539
00:31:23,181 --> 00:31:25,340
Gerd van Rundstedt.

540
00:31:25,341 --> 00:31:27,740
There's a strange situation.

541
00:31:27,741 --> 00:31:31,340
Rommel was inspector of the fortifications.

542
00:31:31,341 --> 00:31:33,980
Van Rundstedt was his boss.

543
00:31:33,981 --> 00:31:36,680
Rommel wanted to fix the front.

544
00:31:36,681 --> 00:31:40,680
Von Nugget was a military of the old school

545
00:31:40,780 --> 00:31:46,420
who believed in mobile warfare with maneuvering armies.

546
00:31:46,520 --> 00:31:48,760
Rommel knew that despite the speed and mobility

547
00:31:48,860 --> 00:31:51,660
of Germany's panzer divisions, allied air power

548
00:31:51,760 --> 00:31:53,760
could easily destroy them.

549
00:31:53,860 --> 00:31:57,500
So he decided to take his case to Hitler.

550
00:31:57,600 --> 00:32:00,540
So my father said we cannot afford

551
00:32:00,640 --> 00:32:04,740
to fight a mobile battle against the British and Americans.

552
00:32:04,741 --> 00:32:10,940
We have to, we should try to win the battle in the first day

553
00:32:10,941 --> 00:32:12,980
at the shore, at the coast.

554
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:15,520
But Hitler never resolved the conflict.

555
00:32:16,380 --> 00:32:19,720
Instead, as he had done before, he used disagreements

556
00:32:19,721 --> 00:32:22,280
between his generals to solidify his own power.

557
00:32:23,060 --> 00:32:25,620
He put himself in charge of the defense plans.

558
00:32:26,340 --> 00:32:29,580
When the invasion came, Hitler would dictate strategy.

559
00:32:30,440 --> 00:32:31,720
Now, Ramos was not a Nazi.

560
00:32:32,580 --> 00:32:33,460
Neither was Ronsted.

561
00:32:33,461 --> 00:32:38,460
Rommel, in fact, was trying to convince Hitler to quit.

562
00:32:38,560 --> 00:32:41,460
Make a surrender now while Germany still got something

563
00:32:41,560 --> 00:32:44,100
to trade.

564
00:32:44,200 --> 00:32:47,000
But Hitler was determined to continue the war.

565
00:32:47,100 --> 00:32:52,720
For him, the war was and always would be all or nothing.

566
00:32:52,820 --> 00:32:55,720
Although the Germans were now aware of the Allied buildup

567
00:32:55,820 --> 00:32:58,760
in England, they still didn't know where the invasion force

568
00:32:58,860 --> 00:33:01,360
would land.

569
00:33:01,361 --> 00:33:04,700
To keep them guessing, the Allies launched an intricate

570
00:33:04,800 --> 00:33:08,160
deception operation called Fortitude.

571
00:33:08,260 --> 00:33:10,800
The plan was designed to trick the Germans into believing

572
00:33:10,900 --> 00:33:16,100
that the invasion would target the Atlantic Wall at Calais.

573
00:33:16,200 --> 00:33:19,940
The way they did that, they created an artificial army

574
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:21,780
group called the First Army Group.

575
00:33:21,880 --> 00:33:24,180
General Patton was there in the United Kingdom,

576
00:33:24,280 --> 00:33:26,780
and he'd been making speeches and getting himself in trouble.

577
00:33:26,880 --> 00:33:29,420
So everybody knew Patton was there.

578
00:33:29,421 --> 00:33:34,420
They put out these rubber fake weapons and fake tanks.

579
00:33:34,520 --> 00:33:39,060
It's just enough so they could be seen from the air.

580
00:33:39,160 --> 00:33:41,160
Another key element of the operation

581
00:33:41,260 --> 00:33:44,000
was spreading misinformation through German spies

582
00:33:44,100 --> 00:33:46,240
in England who had been exposed and coerced

583
00:33:46,340 --> 00:33:48,700
into becoming double agents.

584
00:33:48,800 --> 00:33:51,640
The brigians said, you guys, it's either going

585
00:33:51,740 --> 00:33:53,640
to be a bullet right into your brain

586
00:33:53,740 --> 00:33:55,740
or you're going to work for us.

587
00:33:55,840 --> 00:33:59,420
And these guys said, well, we'll work for you.

588
00:33:59,421 --> 00:34:03,520
The double agents helped convince the Germans of what they already suspected.

589
00:34:04,280 --> 00:34:07,220
The Allies, led by Patton, would attack at Calais.

590
00:34:07,620 --> 00:34:10,420
So how successful was Fortitude? It couldn't have been more successful.

591
00:34:11,600 --> 00:34:13,080
The Allies could not have asked for more.

592
00:34:18,200 --> 00:34:21,320
All the while, the Germans weren't helping their own cause,

593
00:34:21,840 --> 00:34:24,340
as they'd begun to believe their own propaganda films

594
00:34:24,341 --> 00:34:27,280
that celebrated the impenetrability of the Atlantic Wall.

595
00:34:27,281 --> 00:34:32,060
Great photographs taken of the huge guns.

596
00:34:32,400 --> 00:34:33,820
Of course, there weren't very many of them,

597
00:34:33,920 --> 00:34:36,240
but they photographed the same guns many times.

598
00:34:36,660 --> 00:34:38,520
They made sure the Allies were aware of it

599
00:34:38,521 --> 00:34:41,200
and hoped to frighten them, which did not work,

600
00:34:41,640 --> 00:34:44,540
but certainly had great value inside Germany

601
00:34:44,541 --> 00:34:47,300
to show how strong the system was

602
00:34:47,301 --> 00:34:49,400
to hold off the Americans and the British.

603
00:34:50,300 --> 00:34:53,100
Meanwhile, General Eisenhower and the Allies

604
00:34:53,101 --> 00:34:55,220
were making final preparations for D-Day.

605
00:34:55,221 --> 00:34:58,260
Allied troops would land at Normandy's beaches on the

606
00:34:58,360 --> 00:35:02,820
morning of June 5, 1944.

607
00:35:02,920 --> 00:35:06,800
The British would land at Sword and Gold, the Canadians at

608
00:35:06,900 --> 00:35:10,840
Juneau, and the Americans at Omaha and Utah.

609
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:14,280
Every logistic of the most ambitious invasion plan in human

610
00:35:14,380 --> 00:35:18,640
history was falling into place, except for the weather.

611
00:35:18,740 --> 00:35:21,320
Storms were brewing in the English Channel.

612
00:35:21,420 --> 00:35:25,220
Seas were so high that Eisenhower feared the Armageddon

613
00:35:25,221 --> 00:35:27,220
be destroyed before it ever got to France.

614
00:35:30,220 --> 00:35:34,220
And so with everything he heard,

615
00:35:34,320 --> 00:35:36,260
he polled his commanders.

616
00:35:36,360 --> 00:35:38,760
Marty always said, go.

617
00:35:38,860 --> 00:35:40,260
Navy commanders said, no.

618
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:43,780
The Air Force commanders said, no.

619
00:35:43,880 --> 00:35:47,880
So he deferred the invasion.

620
00:35:47,980 --> 00:35:51,580
Ships were called back, planes taken off alert.

621
00:35:51,680 --> 00:35:53,820
If the weather cleared up, Eisenhower

622
00:35:53,821 --> 00:35:56,960
would order them back across the channel to France.

623
00:35:57,060 --> 00:35:59,620
If not, he would have to postpone the invasion

624
00:35:59,720 --> 00:36:03,160
until June 19th to coincide with the next high tide.

625
00:36:07,060 --> 00:36:10,000
On the morning of June 5, Eisenhower's staff

626
00:36:10,100 --> 00:36:13,640
meteorologist promised the weather would improve.

627
00:36:13,740 --> 00:36:16,040
But gray clouds darkened the skies.

628
00:36:16,140 --> 00:36:18,040
He was driving up in a driving rain.

629
00:36:18,140 --> 00:36:20,880
He said, like, how in the world am I ever

630
00:36:20,881 --> 00:36:24,880
going to order an invasion under these conditions.

631
00:36:24,980 --> 00:36:27,020
So he polled.

632
00:36:27,120 --> 00:36:29,380
Once again, Bonnie always said go.

633
00:36:29,480 --> 00:36:31,320
The Navy said, OK.

634
00:36:31,420 --> 00:36:34,020
And the Air Force said, maybe.

635
00:36:34,120 --> 00:36:37,020
So he said, we'll go.

636
00:36:37,120 --> 00:36:40,040
Eisenhower knew there would be no turning back.

637
00:36:40,140 --> 00:36:43,000
More than 150,000 men headed to France

638
00:36:43,100 --> 00:36:46,580
to pierce the Atlantic Wall and push the Germans back to Berlin.

639
00:36:46,581 --> 00:36:50,820
As the sun set on the English Channel,

640
00:36:50,920 --> 00:36:54,820
the Germans had no inkling that the largest invasion force

641
00:36:54,920 --> 00:36:57,420
in history was on its way to Normandy.

642
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:04,400
The term D-Day was first used by the United States Army

643
00:37:04,500 --> 00:37:07,300
during World War I to refer to the first day

644
00:37:07,400 --> 00:37:09,440
of an upcoming battle.

645
00:37:09,540 --> 00:37:13,400
The Atlantic Wall will return on Modern Marvels.

646
00:37:16,580 --> 00:37:23,580
We now return to the Atlantic Wall on Modern Marvels.

647
00:37:23,680 --> 00:37:27,520
June 5, 1944.

648
00:37:27,620 --> 00:37:30,820
After another day bolstering the Atlantic Wall,

649
00:37:30,920 --> 00:37:35,000
German soldiers retired to their quarters.

650
00:37:35,100 --> 00:37:37,460
As they slept in the wall that night,

651
00:37:37,560 --> 00:37:41,000
they had no idea that the invasion had already begun.

652
00:37:41,001 --> 00:37:44,100
begun.

653
00:37:44,200 --> 00:37:47,740
Midnight, June 6th, D-Day.

654
00:37:47,840 --> 00:37:50,740
Soldiers began dropping from the skies behind the coastal

655
00:37:50,840 --> 00:37:53,340
fortifications of Normandy.

656
00:37:53,440 --> 00:37:55,980
Among their most critical assignments were securing

657
00:37:56,080 --> 00:37:59,220
bridges and destroying communication lines.

658
00:37:59,320 --> 00:38:02,820
Paratroopers and glider troops landed well behind the German

659
00:38:02,920 --> 00:38:06,160
lines, but the wall had answers for them, too.

660
00:38:06,260 --> 00:38:11,000
There were poles set in the fields called Rommel's Asparach

661
00:38:11,001 --> 00:38:12,500
to smash up the gliders.

662
00:38:15,900 --> 00:38:19,180
Their fields were flooded wherever the Germans could do

663
00:38:19,280 --> 00:38:22,380
that in order to prevent the troops from landing there.

664
00:38:22,480 --> 00:38:25,480
And at great loss, they did succeed in landing

665
00:38:25,580 --> 00:38:26,380
behind the wall.

666
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:33,620
At dawn, six hours after the commando raids began,

667
00:38:33,720 --> 00:38:35,860
the Germans stationed along the Atlantic Wall

668
00:38:35,960 --> 00:38:39,400
found themselves confronted by the greatest armada ever

669
00:38:39,401 --> 00:38:41,400
assembled.

670
00:38:41,500 --> 00:38:45,400
Suddenly, Normandy shook as the Allied fleet unleashed

671
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:48,040
the full might of its firepower.

672
00:38:48,140 --> 00:38:50,740
Naval gunfire was massive.

673
00:38:50,840 --> 00:38:53,840
The Germans obviously had great difficulties living

674
00:38:53,940 --> 00:38:57,460
through that and surviving it psychologically.

675
00:38:57,560 --> 00:39:01,420
The gunfire included battleship guns up to 14-inch shells

676
00:39:01,520 --> 00:39:03,320
weighing half a ton.

677
00:39:03,420 --> 00:39:04,760
There were cruiser shells.

678
00:39:04,860 --> 00:39:08,600
There were destroyer gunfire, rocket fire.

679
00:39:08,601 --> 00:39:12,140
The beaches were blanketed, but the deep bunkers

680
00:39:12,240 --> 00:39:16,240
were largely safe.

681
00:39:16,340 --> 00:39:18,440
At Sword, where the British would land,

682
00:39:18,540 --> 00:39:21,340
the heavy bombardment was a success.

683
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:24,440
But on other beaches, especially Omaha,

684
00:39:24,540 --> 00:39:27,060
the defenses the Allied soldiers now approached

685
00:39:27,160 --> 00:39:28,660
were still intact.

686
00:39:28,760 --> 00:39:33,260
They got just clobbered, just clobbered by German machine

687
00:39:33,360 --> 00:39:37,200
guns, by German mortars, by German 88s that were flying

688
00:39:37,201 --> 00:39:38,700
across the beach.

689
00:39:38,800 --> 00:39:43,200
And they just took horrendous casualties.

690
00:39:43,300 --> 00:39:45,840
Allied soldiers approaching the shore in Higgins' boats

691
00:39:45,940 --> 00:39:49,540
were caught in the hail of German gunfire.

692
00:39:49,640 --> 00:39:54,420
A shell hit the front end of the boat that I was on

693
00:39:54,520 --> 00:39:56,620
and knocked the ramp right off.

694
00:39:56,720 --> 00:39:59,720
Lieutenant Dixon was sitting in front of me.

695
00:39:59,820 --> 00:40:00,820
And he went down.

696
00:40:00,920 --> 00:40:02,760
And the one in the left compartment

697
00:40:02,860 --> 00:40:05,400
was bleeding around the head.

698
00:40:05,401 --> 00:40:08,640
And he was screaming, get off of the boat.

699
00:40:08,740 --> 00:40:14,740
And Lieutenant Leibowitz, he was the first one I saw got hit.

700
00:40:14,840 --> 00:40:17,780
He was the lieutenant on my boat.

701
00:40:17,880 --> 00:40:19,120
He fell in the water.

702
00:40:19,220 --> 00:40:24,020
I had his head up out of water until he died.

703
00:40:24,120 --> 00:40:26,720
Close to shore, deadly beach obstacles

704
00:40:26,820 --> 00:40:29,120
added to the mayhem.

705
00:40:29,220 --> 00:40:30,820
And we got into the area where there

706
00:40:30,920 --> 00:40:32,620
were the telegraph poles.

707
00:40:32,621 --> 00:40:35,560
And these had teller mines on them.

708
00:40:35,660 --> 00:40:39,160
We dropped down in that area because there was machine

709
00:40:39,260 --> 00:40:40,860
guns firing in the area.

710
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:43,260
And men were being hit.

711
00:40:43,360 --> 00:40:46,060
The soldiers who survived long enough to hit the shore

712
00:40:46,160 --> 00:40:51,280
encountered a barrage of bullets from German machine gun nests.

713
00:40:51,380 --> 00:40:54,080
People followed in all around you.

714
00:40:54,180 --> 00:40:58,320
Well, first sergeant, he got hit.

715
00:40:58,420 --> 00:41:01,140
He turned around, went back to the water,

716
00:41:01,141 --> 00:41:06,520
He said, they'll never take me alive, which they didn't.

717
00:41:06,620 --> 00:41:10,120
At Omaha, there was no place to retreat.

718
00:41:10,220 --> 00:41:14,080
The Americans had no choice but to move forward.

719
00:41:14,180 --> 00:41:17,960
One of the guys started angling towards us

720
00:41:18,060 --> 00:41:20,960
across the beach, and they shot him.

721
00:41:21,060 --> 00:41:23,460
And I saw him hit the dirt.

722
00:41:23,560 --> 00:41:25,860
And he was screaming, you know, for a medic.

723
00:41:25,960 --> 00:41:28,240
And one of our medics went over to help him,

724
00:41:28,340 --> 00:41:29,700
and they shot the medic.

725
00:41:29,701 --> 00:41:31,200
I didn't take more than a few steps

726
00:41:31,300 --> 00:41:33,880
when I was suddenly struck.

727
00:41:33,980 --> 00:41:37,180
Everything in the backpack was shattered

728
00:41:37,280 --> 00:41:39,740
with machine gun bullets.

729
00:41:39,840 --> 00:41:41,980
Wherever soldiers turned, obstacles

730
00:41:42,080 --> 00:41:44,520
and booby traps awaited.

731
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:46,280
He stepped on the wire.

732
00:41:46,380 --> 00:41:49,720
It set it off that bouncing bed.

733
00:41:49,820 --> 00:41:52,060
It's got everything in the book in it.

734
00:41:52,160 --> 00:41:55,020
It's got razor blades, any kind of metal

735
00:41:55,120 --> 00:41:57,300
that they could find to put in there.

736
00:41:57,301 --> 00:42:00,640
I was hit through throat, shoulder, ear.

737
00:42:00,740 --> 00:42:03,200
And it was pretty rough.

738
00:42:03,300 --> 00:42:05,880
Amidst all the carnage, the soldiers at Omaha

739
00:42:05,980 --> 00:42:09,720
reached the bluffs overlooking the shore.

740
00:42:09,820 --> 00:42:14,720
And when I got up to the top, I looked back.

741
00:42:14,820 --> 00:42:17,520
And all I could see was 2,000 ships.

742
00:42:17,620 --> 00:42:21,260
And all I could think was, well, these damn fool Germans,

743
00:42:21,360 --> 00:42:25,460
I wouldn't stay here because we're going to kick their ass.

744
00:42:25,461 --> 00:42:33,200
And then we saw the results of D-Day, and it was appalling.

745
00:42:33,300 --> 00:42:40,240
The bodies were in the surf, rolling in the tide.

746
00:42:40,340 --> 00:42:42,780
And it was a frightful sight.

747
00:42:47,640 --> 00:42:49,760
The assault on the Atlantic Wall at Normandy

748
00:42:49,860 --> 00:42:55,460
on June 6 resulted in some 4,900 Allied casualties.

749
00:42:55,461 --> 00:42:59,700
But ultimately, the wall had been a failure.

750
00:42:59,800 --> 00:43:04,500
The Allies had breached the mighty barrier in just 14 hours.

751
00:43:04,600 --> 00:43:07,040
By August, they were in Paris.

752
00:43:07,140 --> 00:43:11,700
By spring 1945, they'd crossed the Rhine into Germany.

753
00:43:11,800 --> 00:43:14,220
You want to know why the Atlantic wall failed?

754
00:43:14,320 --> 00:43:18,320
Because of the people who went against it.

755
00:43:18,420 --> 00:43:19,820
That's why it failed.

756
00:43:19,821 --> 00:43:23,620
field.

757
00:43:23,720 --> 00:43:27,220
During the six decades since the end of World War II,

758
00:43:27,320 --> 00:43:31,400
much of the wall was dismantled, and most of the guns and steel

759
00:43:31,500 --> 00:43:34,800
salvaged for scrap metal.

760
00:43:34,900 --> 00:43:36,700
Bunkers and obstacles were removed

761
00:43:36,800 --> 00:43:41,880
by those wanting to erase the scars from this barbaric period.

762
00:43:41,980 --> 00:43:44,740
But there are a few places where the twisted visions of a world

763
00:43:44,840 --> 00:43:48,440
gone mad have been preserved.

764
00:43:48,441 --> 00:43:53,440
On the coast of Belgium, a section of the wall is now a museum.

765
00:43:53,540 --> 00:43:59,280
It's a piece of history for us, even if it's a dark page

766
00:43:59,380 --> 00:44:03,480
of history, because many people suffered during the war.

767
00:44:03,580 --> 00:44:07,500
And for that purpose, we want to keep it and preserve it

768
00:44:07,600 --> 00:44:10,000
and show it to everybody, especially

769
00:44:10,100 --> 00:44:13,440
to the young people who do not always

770
00:44:13,540 --> 00:44:16,640
realize that liberty has a certain cost.

771
00:44:18,440 --> 00:44:22,440
Nowhere is that precious cost more apparent than on this

772
00:44:22,540 --> 00:44:24,980
hallowed ground above the beaches of Normandy.

773
00:44:28,980 --> 00:44:34,420
As it turned out, the Atlantic Wall marked the end of an era.

774
00:44:34,520 --> 00:44:38,060
Modern air power and weaponry soon made this kind of fixed

775
00:44:38,160 --> 00:44:40,000
fortification obsolete.

776
00:44:42,200 --> 00:44:46,060
But what remains of the wall serves as a potent reminder

777
00:44:46,061 --> 00:44:50,900
a potent reminder of the tenuous nature of freedom.

